To calculate the new real estate's basis, the taxpayer must identify the given property's adjusted basis ($125,000) and calculate the net amount of boot ($115,000 net received = $40,000 given − $155,000 received).

Boot given | Boot received
Mortgage assumed | $40,000
Cash received |  | $50,000
Vehicle received (FMV) |  | 30,000
Mortgage relief |  | 75,000
Total boot given and received | $40,000 | $155,000

Next, the taxpayer must calculate any recognized gain. Because boot received exceeds boot given, a portion of the property's realized gain was converted into a substantially different asset (cash and vehicle) triggering a recognized gain. Accordingly, the taxpayer must recognize a gain limited to the lesser of the net amount of boot received ($115,000 calculated above) or the realized gain ($250,000 calculated below), which is $115,000.

Realized gain | $250,000 = $375,000 - $125,000

( Note: The deferred gain is $250,000 - $115,000 = $135,000)

After determining the required values, the new real estate's basis can be calculated as follows.

Basis of new property = FMV of property received - deferred gain + deferred loss

$125,000 = $260,000 - $135,000

OR

Acquired property's basis: = / Given property's adjusted basis + boot given − boot received + recognized gain
=: $125,000 + $40,000 − $155,000 + $115,000
=: $125,000

